# Calibration analysis pipeline

Four scripts, run in order. Each reads the previous stage's output and raw
captures are never modified, so every stage can be re-run.

```
capture_calibration.py  ->  runs/run_<timestamp>/           (raw, from the board)
filter_calibration.py   ->  <run>/filtered/                 (corrected + derived columns)
plot_calibration.py     ->  <run>/filtered/plots/*.png      (static figures)
make_replay.py          ->  <run>/filtered/replay.html      (animated replay)
```

Requires `pip install numpy pandas matplotlib pyserial`. The filter, plot
and replay stages expect the original rotation-campaign columns (`targetV`,
`wheel_vel` or `vel_filtered`, `gyroZ_dps`); current firmware dumps use a
different column set (see `runs/INDEX.md`).

## 0. Capture

```
python capture_calibration.py [--port COM5] [--baud 115200] [--outdir <path>]
```

Replaces the serial terminal. Board output is echoed, console input is sent
to the board, and each `--- capture start (test N/M: label) ---` ...
`--- capture end ---` block is saved as `testNN_<label>.csv`. Without
`--port` it tries Bluetooth, then ST-LINK, then other USB ports.

## 1. Filter

```
python filter_calibration.py runs/<run_folder>
```

Gyro bias: the MPU6050 reads about +0.42 dps at rest, which integrates to
about 2 deg over a 5 s test. The bias is measured from phase A of the tests
that start from rest (`from=0.00V`). Tests that spin up first are excluded
because their phase A still contains motion.

Sign convention: the encoder and gyro-Z have opposite positive directions as
mounted, so raw wheel velocity and gyro rate come out positively correlated.
`--flip-gyro` (default on) negates the gyro so both share a convention.

Wheel velocity: for older files `vel_filtered` (`motor.shaft_velocity`) is
used and `vel_raw` ignored, because only the former has SimpleFOC's
`initFOC()` direction correction applied.

Adds `t_s`, `phase` (A/B), `wheel_angle_rad`, `wheel_accel`, `gyro_dps`,
`platform_deg`, and `power_mW` / `iq_est_A` when current was logged, plus a
per-test `summary.csv`. Options: `--no-flip-gyro`, `--bias <dps>`,
`--iq-min-v <V>`, `--settle-dps <dps>`, `--outdir <path>`.

## 2. Plot

```
python plot_calibration.py runs/<run_folder>/filtered
```

- `test<NN>_*.png`: command, wheel velocity, platform rate and heading on a
  shared time axis, with the phase A/B boundary marked.
- `overview_steps.png`: from-rest steps overlaid, aligned at the step.
- `overview_scaling.png`: steady-state wheel velocity and peak platform rate
  vs command. Points near a line through the origin mean a linear model is
  reasonable.

## 3. Replay

```
python make_replay.py runs/<run_folder>/filtered
```

A single self-contained HTML file: top-down view of the platform and wheel
driven by the recorded data, telemetry readouts, momentum bars, scrubbable
traces and a test selector. Spokes alias at speed, so read the numeric
velocity. The momentum bars are scaled per body, not a conserved total,
since `J_w` and `J_p` are not measured. Options: `--out <path>`,
`--frames <n>` (default 500 per test).

## Notes on the 2026-07-29 run

About 320 Hz realized log rate with under 100 us of jitter, all nine tests
ended on `platform_settled`, and peak wheel velocity scaled near-linearly
with command (7.7 to 8.3 rad/s per volt over a 4x range).

The 3.0 V hold shows the platform ringing (a decaying swing through zero in
the last ~175 ms of phase A), so larger disturbances may need a longer
settle time than the fixed hold.
